# Q20 round-2 patch (idempotent): replaces Q20a-d with revised Q20a-g.  python3 -I build_report_q20b.py
import json
p='/output/report.json'; r=json.load(open(p))
r['results']=[x for x in r['results'] if not x['quantity'].startswith('Q20')]
A=lambda n,v:{"name":n,"value":v}
com=[A("model","one-zone uniform-sphere RADEX LVG (LAMDA HCN, HCO+; dv=150 km/s, N/dv not rescaled), grids out/q4c/grid4_*.npz, Tbg 2.73 and 5 K; flat priors log T 10-150 K, log n 3-8, log N 13-18.5; 1.7 arcsec ladder data of Q12b (Harada+2018 1-0 ratios as labelled assumption)"),
     A("filling-factor convention","eta_core = fill inside the Q5 Gaussian core at 0.24 arcsec; eta_b = source area/beam area for a named beam = eta_core x s^2/(s^2+b^2) (s core FWHM, b beam FWHM); D=40 Mpc, 1 arcsec=194 pc"),
     A("independence","10 arcsec intensities are team-b p0028 values; aperture/deblend is not independent of ours")]
def add(q,t,v,u,un,c,e): r['results'].append({"quantity":q,"target":t,"value":v,"uncertainty":un,"unit":u,"confidence":c,"evidence":e,"assumptions":com})
add("Q20a: aperture-referenced filling factors: the S cool core has eta_core~0.5-0.8 at 0.24 arcsec, which IS eta_1.7~0.01-0.02 (p0012-r02's 'eta~0.01') and eta_10~5e-4: the two statements are the same solution",
 "S core, N core",
 "Chain eta_b = eta_core x s^2/(s^2+b^2) (out/q20/q20g_eta_chain.csv). Q5 Gaussian cores: S s=0.25-0.34\" (49-66 pc), N 0.26-0.29\" (50-56 pc). Geometric dilution alone (eta_core=1): eta_1.7=0.021-0.038 (S), 0.023-0.028 (N); eta_10=6e-4-1.2e-3. Floor/unresolved case s=0.10\": eta_1.7=0.003, eta_10=1e-4. S cool fit (Tint tied, 1z, Tbg 2.73): T=19.6 [16.9,24.6] K, eta_core=0.61 [0.49,0.75] -> eta_1.7 = 0.013 (s=0.25\") to 0.023 (s=0.34\"), eta_10=(4-7)e-4. Conversely eta_1.7=0.01-0.02 needs eta_core=0.47-0.94 (s=0.25\") or 0.26-0.52 (s=0.34\"). p0012-r02's eta~0.01 is therefore REPRODUCED as a 1.7\" aperture filling (dilute in the 1.7\" beam, nearly space-filling inside the 50-60 pc core); it is NOT a dilute core at 0.24\". p0028's eta>=0.05 refers to a 10\" aperture, where a 50-pc core alone has eta_10<=1e-3, so p0028's constraint concerns the envelope (Q20d), not the core. Table (S, eta_core 0.2/0.6/1.0): s=0.25\": eta_1.7 0.004/0.013/0.021, eta_10 1e-4/4e-4/6e-4; s=0.34\": 0.008/0.023/0.039, 2e-4/7e-4/1.2e-3. Lower bound from the 0.24\" T_b (Tex<=Tk): eta_core >= T_core/[J(Tk)-J(Tbg)] with T_core=Tb_025/dil(s). S (HCN/HCO+), s=0.25\": Tk=20 K 0.18/0.40, 30 K 0.11/0.24, 80 K 0.03/0.07; s=0.34\": 20 K 0.14/0.31. N s=0.27\": 20 K 0.36/0.38, 30 K 0.21/0.23. A SMALLER size raises the bound: s=0.15\" S HCO+ 0.74 at 20 K; s=0.10\" (floor, T_core S HCN/HCO+ 9.1/19.9 K, N 17.8/19.1 K) needs Tk>=15/26 K (S) and 24/25 K (N) even at eta_core=1, eta_core>=1.4 at 20 K for S HCO+ (impossible): a 20 K S core requires a RESOLVED core s>=~0.17\" (>=33 pc). The size interval is thus the dominant uncertainty of eta_core, but it only matters at the small-size end (Q20c below).",
 "dimensionless","size 0.25-0.34\" (S), 0.26-0.29\" (N); Tint/Tcore +-30%","medium",
 "scripts/q20g_phys.py -> out/q20/q20g_phys.log, q20g_eta_chain.csv; scripts/q20d_bounds.py -> q20d_eta_min.csv")
add("Q20b: S Tkin<80 K at 1.7 arcsec persists for free eta; the tied-eta S interval WITH the size interval; N tied fits are not used as support; sub-thermal single phase needs n~2-3e4 and is disfavoured (dchi2~6)",
 "S, N (1.7 arcsec deblended ladders)",
 "(i) eta fully free (ratio-only r8): S T=19.5 [16.8,24.7] K, P(T>=80)=0.00; eta_HCO+/eta_HCN free within x10: 19.9 [13.8,31] K; N 61 [38,94] K (P>=80 0.27-0.34), N-S dT not significant (as Q12). (ii) S tied to Tint, including the Q5 size interval (Tint scaled with size, out/q20/runs/tsc*): pedestal-inclusive s~0.45\" (Tint 1.7/3.7 K): T=19.6 [16.9,24.6], eta 0.61 [0.49,0.75], chi2 5.4/5; Q5 Gaussian core (x1.5, s~0.27\"): T=21 [18,26] K, eta 0.82 [0.68,0.93], chi2 5.6/5 (p=0.35); s~0.14\" (x2.5): T=22 [19,26], eta 0.93 [0.84,0.98], chi2 9.0/5; floor s~0.10\" (x4): T=22 [20,26], eta 0.95, chi2 19.8/5 (p=0.001, rejected). So the tight eta interval [0.49,0.77] was conditional on the pedestal size: over the Q5 Gaussian range the interval is eta_core 0.5-0.95 (eta_1.7=0.013-0.04), T stays 17-26 K for every size the data allow. (iii) N: tied fits (Tint tied, eta 0.91, T 37 [26,55] K, chi2 11.2/5, p=0.05) are NOT support: pulls are +2.4 and +1.8 sigma on the HCN and HCO+ 3-2/2-1 ratios, i.e. the Tint datum (3.3 K, needs eta<~0.1 at T>60 K or eta~0.9 at 37 K) fights the ladder, and the tension worsens with the Q5 Gaussian size (chi2 17.1/5, 30.7, 45.1 for x1.5, 2.5, 4): the N ladder is better described by the ratio-only warm solution (T 61 [38,94], eta unconstrained). N Tk is therefore from the ladder only. (iv) Sub-thermal single phase (Tex<Tk): forcing Tk>=50 K gives S T=55 [51,63] K, n=2.6e4 [1.7e4,1e5] cm-3, eta_core 0.78 [0.62,0.92], tau32(HCN)~29, chi2 11.5 (dof 5; ratios-only 11.5/4, p=0.02-0.04) vs 5.4 for the free fit: dchi2~6 (2.4 sigma), disfavoured not excluded; N Tk>=50 K: T 60-74, n=(0.4-0.8)e5, chi2 11.6/5 (tied), 9.2/4 (ratio-only, as good as free fit). With Tex<Tk the eta lower bound is STRONGER (J(Tex)<J(Tk): e.g. S HCO+ at Tex=20 K, s=0.25\": eta>=0.40 whatever Tk). S upper limit depends on the 4-3/3-2 deblend (p0012-r01).",
 "K / dimensionless","16-84% intervals (SMC, 2 seeds)","medium",
 "scripts/q20b_eta.py (env Q20_TSC size scaling, Q20_TMIN), q20_run.sh, q20h_run.sh -> out/q20/runs/")
add("Q20c: p0028 'Tk lower limit survives free eta' is SUPPORTED at P(T>50 K)=0.6-0.9 for realistic correlated errors (not 'confirmed'); the quoted dchi2 36-41 against 25 K is not reproduced (5-15)",
 "N, S (10 arcsec apertures)",
 "Error model (scripts/q20f_10arcsec_corr.py): per-line independent error = 3% stat (+) aperture/beam-matching (2-1: 10%, as the 9.4x6.2\" B5 beam is comparable to the 10\" aperture and loses extended flux; 3-2: 3%; 4-3: 5%), plus a common per-band flux-scale factor (B5, B6, B7; Gaussian 10% in ln, shared by HCN and HCO+, marginalised analytically via the covariance). ALMA B5-B7 absolute flux accuracy is ~10% per band, hence sigma_g=10%; the 7% case is dropped. Results (p0028 intensities; shared eta / separate eta; Tbg 2.73 K -> Tbg 5 K): baseline (sigma_g 10%, aperture x1): N T=100 [80,134] / 92 [52,128] K, P(T>50)=0.93/0.85 (Tbg 5: 0.79/0.77), P(T<=30)=0.05/0.07 (0.11/0.08); S T=79 [57,93] / 81 [58,94] K, P(T>50)=0.90/0.91 (0.87/0.83), P(T<=30)=0.06/0.04 (0.09/0.05). Aperture error x2: P(T>50)=0.71-0.77 (Tbg 2.73), 0.63-0.66 (Tbg 5); P(T<=30)=0.11-0.16 (0.13-0.21). sigma_g=15%: P(T>50)=0.75-0.83 (0.64-0.76). Old uncorrelated 15% (reference): 0.71-0.78 (0.57-0.67). So the warm 10\" solution is supported at P(T>50)=0.6-0.9, P(T<=30)=0.04-0.2, depending on Tbg and the aperture error, with eta_med 0.05-0.1 (area factor of the 10\" aperture, tau32(HCN) 24-80 = very thick). Cold-gas test (eta>=0.05, T=25 K): dchi2 relative to best = 5.7 (S sep) to 15 (S shared), 4.4 (N sep) to 9.6 (N shared) at baseline errors, versus 36-41 quoted by p0028: p0028's cold-gas exclusion is therefore NOT reproduced as a >5 sigma result; it is a 2-4 sigma preference. Verdict: Tk lower limit of ~50 K at 10\": supported (probability 0.6-0.9), REVISED in strength. It constrains the envelope: the core share of the 10\" 3-2 flux is 4-11% (Gaussian core) to 10-28% (pedestal-inclusive) (Q20e).",
 "K / probability","posterior 16-84%; error-model range quoted","medium",
 "scripts/q20f_10arcsec_corr.py -> out/q20/q20f_*.log (0.10_1_0 baseline, 0.10_2_0 aperture x2, 0.15_1_0, 0.10_1_1 old 15% uncorrelated); q20c_10arcsec.py (old)")
add("Q20d: external Tk floor from CO(2-1) at 55 pc (Brunetti+2021) and from the 1.2 mm continuum: consistent with the S 17-25 K core only if eta_CO>~0.5-0.9; the 10\" 'warm' solution is not tested",
 "S, N",
 "Brunetti+2021 (MNRAS 500, 4730; arXiv 2011.01250) Table 2, CO(2-1) at 0.25\" (55 pc): peak T_b (16/50/84th percentiles) northern nucleus 5.1/11/20 K, southern nucleus 6.5/13/18 K; map maximum 37 K; Tpk of optically thick CO traces Tk (their Sec 5.1: a lower limit once beam filling is included); sigma 42-49 km/s, Sigma 870/1700 Msun/pc2 (N/S), Pturb 55 pc: N (1.6-8.2)e8, S (3.3-12)e8 K cm-3. Table values read from the arXiv HTML (garbled row labels; values for the two nuclei unambiguous), not verified in the published PDF. Test Tk>=Tpk/eta_CO: with J_CO(2-1)(Tk)-J(Tbg)= 11.9/14.8/19.7/24.6 K at Tk=17/20/25/30: S median 13 K needs eta_CO>=0.88 (Tk 20 K), 0.66 (25 K), 0.53 (30 K); 84th percentile 18 K needs 1.2 (20 K), 0.9 (25 K); S max 37 K needs Tk>=40 K. N median 11 K: 0.74 (20 K), 0.45 (30 K); 84th 20 K: >1 (Tk<25 K). So the S 17-25 K solution (eta_core 0.5-0.8) is consistent with the median CO nuclear T_b if eta_CO~0.7-1, uncomfortable at the 84th percentile/maximum, which require Tk>=25-40 K; it is NOT excluded and is exactly what eta_core~0.6 predicts (CO and HCN cores are both ~0.25\"). Caveat: Brunetti's pixel Tpk is the T_b per 55 pc beam, and the S/N nuclear positions are not given (percentiles are over the nuclear region); our S core may sit below the peak. The N solution (Tk 37-100 K) is unconstrained by CO. The 10\" warm solution (Tk 60-100 K) is consistent (CO bound only a floor). 1.2 mm continuum T_b at 0.24\" (Xb4b cont, 257.8 GHz, scripts/q20e_cont_tb.py): peak 0.56 mJy/b (N) and 1.85 mJy/b (S) = RJ 0.18 and 0.59 K: a trivial Td lower bound of 3-4 K (optically thin dust, tau_1.2mm 0.004-0.04 for Td 20-80 K), no Tk constraint. It does give a dust column cross-check: beam-averaged N_H2(0.24\")= (0.9-2.7)e23 (N) and (1.7-8.9)e23 (S) cm-2 for Td 80-20 K, kappa_1.2mm=1 cm2/g, GDR 100 (labelled).",
 "K","Table 2 percentiles; dust column +-factor 2 (kappa, GDR)","medium",
 "scripts/q20g_phys.py (CO), scripts/q20e_cont_tb.py; Brunetti+2021 Table 2")
add("Q20e: physical quantities of the S/N core solutions (n, N, N_H2 for labelled X, path length, core mass, nT, tau, H13CN check) and the 10\" envelope",
 "S, N cores (1.7\" fits with Tint; Tbg 2.73 K)",
 "Median [16,84] (out/q20/q20g_phys.csv). S (Tint tied, pedestal size): T=19.6 [16.9,24.6] K; n(H2)=1.1e5 [4.7e4,2.2e5] cm-3; log N(HCN)=16.58 [16.19,16.90], log N(HCO+)=16.09 [15.69,16.42] (N/dv with dv=150 km/s; X(HCN)/X(HCO+)=3.2 [2.6,3.6]); tau32(HCN)=39 [16,84], tau32(HCO+)=19 [8,40], tau43(HCN)=12 [4.7,26]; nT=2.1e6 [1.0,4.7]e6 K cm-3; eta_core 0.61 [0.49,0.75]. With X(HCN)=1e-8 (assumed; X(HCO+)=3e-9): N_H2=3.8e24 [1.5,8]e24 cm-2, path length N_H2/n = 11 [2,56] pc (<=core size 50-66 pc, so uniform-sphere is self-consistent only for the lower half of the posterior), gas mass inside the Gaussian core (1.133 s^2, s=0.25\") = 1.3e8 [0.6,2.8]e8 Msun (scales as 1e-8/X). Compare Mdyn(S, 48 pc) = 0.58 (0.41-0.78)e8 (x2 systematic; 1.3e8 with pressure term) from Q5f: X(HCN)=1e-8 gives M_gas ~ 1-2 Mdyn, X=1e-9 gives 10-20 Mdyn (rejected); the dust cross-check (beam-average N_H2 (0.5-0.9)e24 at Td 20-30 K x eta_core 0.6 x dil 0.52) compares with the molecular beam-averaged 1.2e24 for X=1e-8 (factor ~2) and 1e25 for X=1e-9 (excluded): X(HCN)>~1e-8 is required. Pressure: nT(S)~2e6, N 4.6e6 [4,5.4e6] K cm-3 vs Brunetti Pturb(55 pc) 3e8-1e9 (S), 2e8-8e8 (N): the thermal pressure is 2-3 orders below the turbulent one (turbulence-dominated, as in Brunetti). N (Tint tied; not used as support, see Q20b): T 37 [26,55] K, n=1.2e5, N(HCN)=1.6e16, tau32 HCN 17 / HCO+ 2.8, nT=4.6e6, eta 0.91, M_gas(X=1e-8)=1.0e8 Msun; ratio-only N: T 61 [38,94] K, n=6.5e4, tau32 HCN 11, HCO+ 2.0. 10\" envelope (Q20c): T 70-100 K, eta_10~0.05-0.1, tau32(HCN)=24-80 (thick; n,N not tabulated; a column fixed by eta_10 alone is not constrained). H13CN check (HCN/H13CN(4-3) > 4 at S, >8.3 at N from Q12; flux ratio (1-e^-tau)/(1-e^-tau/R) with 12C/13C=R): fraction of the S posterior consistent with the limit: P=0.39/0.59/0.90 for R=30/50/100 (median tau43 HCN 12 [4.7,26]); N tied: 0.11/0.70/1.0 (tau43 5.5). So the H13CN limit mildly disfavours the thickest S solutions (tau43>~15-25) but allows tau32~10-40 for R>=50; a 'thick core' (tau>>1) is required by the ratios, tau~5-25 is supported by H13CN.",
 "mixed","16-84% posterior; X, kappa assumptions labelled; factor ~2 systematic on masses","low",
 "scripts/q20g_phys.py -> out/q20/q20g_phys.log, q20g_phys.csv")
add("Q20f: is the S cool solution a dilute thick core inside a warmer envelope (p0012-r02)? Yes in structure; the core is dilute only in the 1.7/10 arcsec beams, not at 0.24 arcsec",
 "N, S",
 "Core share of the 10\" 3-2 flux (Tint x dilution x 1.0645 FWHM_v): pedestal-inclusive size (upper bound) N HCN 28%, HCO+ 17%, S 10% both; Q5 Gaussian cores N 11%/6%, S 5%/4%; subtracting a core with the 1.7\" ladder changes the 10\" ratios by <=0.02, so the 10\" ladder measures the envelope (>=70-95% of the flux) and p0028's cold-gas test does not test the S core. Verdict on p0012-r02: SUPPORTED: S gas at 1.7\" is cool (17-25 K, eta_core 0.5-0.9 -> eta_1.7 0.01-0.04, the same as the proposed eta~0.01), inside a 10\" envelope that is warmer (Tk 60-100 K, P(T>50)=0.6-0.9, eta_10 0.05-0.1). The earlier phrasing 'revised on eta' is withdrawn (Q20a). What remains open: (a) a sub-thermal single phase with Tk>=50 K and n~2-3e4 is disfavoured only at dchi2~6 (Q20b); (b) the cool-core Tk rests on the 1.7\" 4-3/3-2 deblend (p0012-r01) and a resolved core (s>=0.17\"); (c) CO T_b at the S core is consistent if eta_CO~0.7-1 (Q20d); (d) IR pumping/Tbg: Tbg 5 K shifts T by <=5 K, HCN v2=1 not detected (not tested further). Tk-eta degeneracy: T_b alone allows warm S gas with eta_core>=0.03-0.1 (Tk 50-150 K), so the T_b bound plus the ladders, not T_b alone, make the cool solution.",
 "fraction / K","core share +-30%; not independent of Q12/q1 deblend","low",
 "scripts/q20d_bounds.py -> out/q20/q20d_bounds.log, q20d_core_share10.csv; Q20a-e")
